"""Focused responsibility/continuity pass; native assignments, no grade engine."""
SCHOOLS = {
 'Phoenix Wing': dict(teacher='Haruki Saitō',head='Kōhei Sanada',assessment='Phoenix Wing: Hold the Line',subject='a supervised defensive practical',
  description='Begin your first year at Phoenix Wing Academy in Glowhaven. Meet classmates with something to prove and prepare for a practical where protecting your partner matters more than looking impressive.',
  goal='Prepare for and take part in the first defensive practical, then hear the instructor’s feedback.',
  public='Ritsu Kurogane’s first defensive practical will ask each pair to keep a partner safe through a controlled disruption. Prepare an approach you can actually perform, agree roles with a willing partner, and show what happens when the plan meets resistance.',
  where='Training Grounds',examiner='Ritsu Kurogane',
  brief='Competence and responsibility. Haruki introduces the principles; Ritsu runs a supervised beginner practical. Choose a consenting available partner; Hinae Komori is a possibility, not a forced recruit. Protecting position, using cover, giving a clear warning, interrupting safely or managing a withdrawal can be as useful as a barrier. No advanced spell or victory over a level-15 classmate is required. A staged protected target can replace a partner if necessary. Give the partner an actual preference or difficulty. A flashy solo finish that leaves the protected person exposed is criticized. Honest mistakes permit a correction and new attempt; careless endangerment has consequences. Assess preparation, control and response to the partner, not damage output. This does not replace the existing team-arena job The Line Behind You.',
  scene='''Red pennants snap above Phoenix Wing’s gate. Older students stream past with practice bags and breakfast rolls; the newcomers slow at every doorway, unwilling to be the first to admit they have lost the signs. Behind the training wall, a whistle cuts through the clack of wooden weapons.

At the Main Hall desk, a clerk checks the new arrival against the admitted list and hands over a folded timetable. A red line beneath the morning subjects marks an afternoon preparation period. At the bottom, in heavier ink: FIRST DEFENSIVE PRACTICAL — PARTNER WORK.

Hinae Komori is still looking at that line when Nemu Usami leans over her shoulder.

“At least they’re telling us in advance,” Hinae says. “I’d like someone to tell me what they mean by ‘under pressure.’”

“Probably not a comfortable chair,” Nemu replies. Her rabbit ears twitch toward the training wall. “I intend to raise an objection.”

Kokona Hanamori arrives with a small bag of sweets and offers one to Hinae. Nemu reaches for it first. “I’m arranging a preparation table,” Kokona says. “If you’re going to sleep through it, at least don’t take the last one.”

Riko Umezawa pauses on her way to a returning-year session. “You’re spending an entire afternoon on the first practice?”

“Some of us need the practice,” Hinae says.

“And some of us need the sweets,” Nemu adds. Riko laughs, checks her own room number and hurries on before anyone can ask how much preparation she has done.

The welcome bell gathers the first-years into the hall. Kōhei Sanada waits for the last bench to stop scraping before he speaks.

“You will study Magic Theory, Magical Combat, and Potions and Alchemy. Attend your scheduled lessons. Prepare the work we assign. Talent excuses neither absence nor putting another person in danger.”

He lays one hand on the lectern.

“Your first practical is about protecting someone. You may discover that keeping a partner safe is less flattering than winning a duel. Learn the difference. Your instructors will tell you what needs improving, and you will have a chance to improve it.”

Outside the east doors, Takeru Akabane is fastening a dueling-club notice to the board. The second-year catches a falling tack before it reaches the floor. “Open practice after the preparation block,” he tells the passing group. “Watch, try a drill, see whether you like it. You don’t have to join to show up.”

The headmaster returns toward Faculty Offices while the first-years find the Lecture Hall. Haruki Saitō has placed a wooden screen, a chalk line and a padded target near the front. He checks the stopper on his travel mug before lifting the screen far enough to reveal a gap at its base.

“A shield can be quite sound,” he says, “and still leave somebody unprotected. Start with what you can observe. What would you check before trusting this position?”

Hinae crouches far enough to examine the gap without crossing the chalk. “Nemu, can you see the target from your side?”

Nemu studies the distance to the nearest bench. “I can supervise.”

The screen is still raised. Several students move for a better view; others are already disagreeing about the safest place to stand. There is room to inspect, speak or join the exercise.'''),
 'Gryffin Tail': dict(teacher='Kei Aoki',head='Norio Shibata',assessment='Gryffin Tail: A Claim Worth Testing',subject='a short research demonstration',
  description='Begin your first year at Gryffin Tail Academy in Glowhaven. A puzzling demonstration introduces your classmates and the first question you will have to defend with evidence.',
  goal='Develop a modest testable question, demonstrate or investigate it, and discuss the result with the instructor.',
  public='Kei Aoki has asked the first-years for a small working hypothesis and an honest test. A clear negative result can be good work. Decide what you want to find out, prepare a feasible method, and show what the evidence supports.',
  where='Lecture Hall',examiner='Kei Aoki',
  brief='Intellectual achievement. A beginner can investigate a mundane observation, test an existing cantrip, compare materials, examine a harmless device or document a controlled failure. No original spell or advanced engineering is compulsory. Nahlah Qamar can offer careful observation; Tatsuo Nagase wants a useful result and dislikes expensive materials. Satoru Kanda is a second-year helper, not a fellow first-year, and has his own difficulty with practical performance. Give peers different claims rather than telling the player which hypothesis is correct. Credit a suitable method and an honest interpretation even if the hypothesis fails. A polished unsupported claim, hidden result or fabricated measurement is a real problem. A short demonstration and discussion suffice; no obligatory essay, purchase, guild job or expedition. This is distinct from the existing Blackboard and Polish Versus Proof situations.',
  scene='''A box of brass fittings rattles past Gryffin Tail’s entrance on a handcart. Two returning students argue over whether the noise is normal. Neither slows down. Blue banners move above the courtyard, and the first-years cluster near the Main Hall desk with the concentrated expressions of people trying to look at home.

The clerk hands the new arrival a timetable. Morning subjects fill the first block; a preparation session follows later in the day. A separate notice names the first demonstration: A CLAIM WORTH TESTING.

Nahlah Qamar has caught a beetle on the back of her hand. Her turquoise eyes follow its legs as it negotiates a seam in her cuff.

“It keeps choosing the darker edge,” she says. “Or perhaps that’s simply where I keep tilting my hand. I haven’t decided.”

Tatsuo Nagase peers over. “If we’re studying insects, can the question be whether they eat the grain in my lunch?”

“You would need to let it near your lunch.”

He considers the cost and steps back.

Norio Shibata’s welcome begins without a grand flourish. He names Magic Theory, Magical Engineering and Magical Arts, then lifts one of the demonstration notices.

“You are here to become capable of finding things out. An impressive explanation is useful only if it survives contact with the thing explained. Bring a question you can actually test. Tell us what happened, including the part you hoped would go differently.”

He makes the attendance and preparation requirements plain. Students who struggle will receive specific work to improve; the school will not accept invented results in place of that work.

At the corridor board, Satoru Kanda is arranging a small display for a second-year study circle. His diagram is immaculate. The little rotor beneath it refuses to turn. Renji Hanabusa has already written an enthusiastic description of the finished demonstration.

“That is a loose contact,” Satoru says before anyone asks.

“Then the description is accurate in principle,” Renji replies.

Mitsuki Amano sets her crystal sphere beyond the rotor’s reach. “If either of you knocks this off the stand, your next experiment can be putting it back together.” They are returning second-years, and their own work is clearly not finished. The open-session notice invites first-years to bring questions after their preparation block.

The headmaster heads back to Faculty Offices. Inside the Lecture Hall, Kei Aoki has set two apparently identical glass vessels on the desk. A narrow streamer moves beside one and hangs motionless beside the other.

“You can ask to inspect them,” Kei tells the room. “You can propose a test. You cannot tell me you know what is inside because you prefer one explanation.”

Nahlah compares the vessels from the edge of her table. Tatsuo looks at the streamer, then tries to see beneath the desk without leaving his place.

“I’d like to know whether we’re allowed to touch the table.”

Two students behind them propose different explanations at once. Kei asks which observation would distinguish them. The vessels remain available for a closer look.'''),
 'Dragon Hoard': dict(teacher='Noriaki Takatsuki',head='Miyu Asahina',assessment='Dragon Hoard: Earn Your Place',subject='a supervised field-readiness exercise',
  description='Begin your first year at Dragon Hoard Academy in Glowhaven. Expedition places are coveted, and your first exercise asks whether you can recognize a discovery without mishandling it.',
  goal='Prepare a safe approach to the training find, complete the supervised readiness exercise, and discuss the result.',
  public='Madoka Narukami is assessing how first-years observe, handle uncertainty and work with a partner before later expedition placements are considered. Prepare a sensible plan for a supervised training find, then show how you respond when its condition differs from the notes.',
  where='Training Grounds',examiner='Madoka Narukami',
  brief='Prestige and proven field ability. This is a safe local readiness exercise with a clearly identified training object, not an actual foreign expedition, the level-20 field test or a court-key exam. No portal permission or foreign place is granted. The player can observe, secure the surroundings, request help, identify limits or choose not to touch an uncertain object. Reward a justified stop as readily as a safe recovery. Existing techniques and ordinary tools suffice. Suzune Mikazuki has useful defensive judgment but resents being reduced to her shield; Chiyo Tsukikage is theatrical without secret powers; Yūji Nishimura wants to be useful despite ordinary ability. Ryūka Marufuji is a second-year who can criticize, advise or compete according to the actual exchange. She need not become a friend or an unavoidable rival. Strong work earns credible attention for later opportunities, not a guaranteed scarce expedition berth. Reckless handling, cheating or putting someone at risk affects standing.',
  scene='''Yellow-and-gold banners hang over Dragon Hoard’s orderly entrance. Beyond the first court, porters are checking sealed equipment cases against a list. A group of older students waits beside them, trying to appear less interested in the names on the expedition notice than they plainly are.

The registrar finds the new arrival’s name and supplies a first-year timetable. Alongside the required morning subjects is a preparation block for a supervised field-readiness exercise. No distant destination is promised. The notice says that instructors will remember how students handle an uncertain find.

Chiyo Tsukikage reads the heading with grave satisfaction.

“At last. The first seal upon the path of forbidden—”

“It says training object,” Suzune Mikazuki interrupts. She has turned her broad shield to avoid blocking the desk. “I’d like to see the handling instructions before anybody breaks a seal.”

Yūji Nishimura checks the two papers in his own hands. “If one of you needs someone to carry the notes, I can do that. Or something more useful. Either.”

The first-years are called into the welcome. Miyu Asahina lets her eyes travel over their untidy row before she begins.

“Magic Theory, Spellcraft and Magical Creatures form your core timetable. You will prepare for them. Our expeditions are not prizes for whoever reaches an object first. Other people must be able to trust your observations, your hands and your account of what happened.”

She gestures toward the cases beyond the court.

“There are opportunities here worth working for. There are also people prepared to mistake attention for achievement. Learn to distinguish them in yourselves.”

In the corridor afterward, Ryūka Marufuji is studying the expedition notice while Ema Tsunoda sorts colored sample labels beside her. Both are second-years with their own preparations to finish.

“Those could have been plain,” Ryūka says.

“And someone could have mixed them up in bad light,” Ema replies, holding the labels against each other. “Are you helping me test them, or giving another opinion?”

Ryūka takes a pair with a small, sharp smile. “Apparently both.”

A nearby creature-observation group has advertised an open session after preparation; its demonstration remains optional. Miyu returns to Faculty Offices, and the first-years reach Noriaki Takatsuki’s Lecture Hall.

On the front table sits a closed training case beside a sketch of what it supposedly contains. The fastening on the actual case faces a different way.

“Before we discuss the spell,” Noriaki says, “tell me what you would establish before opening this.”

Chiyo has already noticed the mismatch and is arguing that it must be deliberate. Suzune wants to know how the case was transported. Yūji begins comparing the sketch with the fastening. Their discussion leaves several ways for another student to contribute—or challenge the premise.'''),
 'Hydra Head': dict(teacher='Kana Fujimoto',head='Kiyoko Fujinami',assessment='Hydra Head: A Settlement That Holds',subject='a civic negotiation exercise',
  description='Begin your first year at Hydra Head Academy in Glowhaven. Classmates and older helpers draw you into a practical dispute where a persuasive answer still has to work for the people involved.',
  goal='Prepare for the civic exercise, take part in a workable proposal, and hear feedback on its effects and limits.',
  public='The first-years will negotiate a shared worksite in a supervised civic exercise. Several people have legitimate needs and limited resources. Find out what they actually require, test a proposal against the practical facts, and explain what remains unresolved.',
  where='Lecture Hall',examiner='Kana Fujimoto',
  brief='Reputation, diplomacy and practical influence. Kana frames the theory; Reon’s Enchantment and Kaoruko’s Herbology provide the worksite’s real constraints. Do not invent a new core Diplomacy professor. The exercise uses assigned roles, actual limits and a modest enchanted garden/work area, not a real city emergency. Strong persuasion cannot create missing water, free labor or consent. A clear partial agreement, documented disagreement or justified alternative can be credible work; universal harmony is not required. Allow directness, listening, practical craft, evidence, negotiation and refusing an unfair premise. The novice need not defeat an older duelist or use an unlearned discipline. Homura Hibino, Gaku Inukai and Bunta Ushijima are second-year helpers with their own interests, not first-year classmates. Use actual first-year incidental students without inventing replacements for established named roles. Assess preparation, credible terms and follow-through; deception that is discovered and promises knowingly made impossible affect standing.',
  scene='''Violet cloth stirs above Hydra Head’s court. Someone is moving planters away from a doorway while a second student insists that the sign saying KEEP CLEAR refers to the other doorway. The queue of new arrivals divides around them and reforms at the Main Hall desk.

The clerk supplies a timetable and a short preparation notice. Morning classes are required. An afternoon work session precedes the first civic exercise: a shared garden and practice space must serve several groups with different needs.

Gaku Inukai, wearing a second-year helper’s ribbon, is offering to organize the new students’ preparation groups.

“One list, everything settled. Very efficient.”

Bunta Ushijima holds the list upside down. “Do they know what you’ve promised them?”

“They will when we have enough people.”

Near the planters, Homura Hibino lowers a rolled diagram and examines the practice-space notice. “If nobody uses the east corner, it would be ideal for a small demonstration.”

“How small?” Bunta asks.

“That depends on whether they give me the east corner.”

Asuka Tsurugi, a third-year on her way to practice, stops to point out that the east corner is beside the dueling approach. Fourth-year Tamaki Kiryū checks the bookings rather than joining the argument.

“You can have the demonstration after the practice ends,” Tamaki says. “If the smoke clears before my group arrives.”

“There doesn’t have to be smoke,” Homura objects, with insufficient conviction. None of them has abandoned their own morning for the newcomers.

The welcome bell intervenes. The older helpers step aside as the first-years gather before Kiyoko Fujinami. She names Magic Theory, Enchantment and Herbology and makes the timetable’s obligations clear.

“People will ask what your education is worth when something needs to work,” she says. “An agreement is not useful because it sounds gracious. Someone must be able to carry it out. You will prepare your proposals, listen to objections, and learn what your promises cost other people.”

She does not promise an answer that satisfies everyone. Students will receive feedback, and work that fails can be revised. Ignoring the people affected by it is another matter.

In the corridor, a notice invites newcomers to an open performance-and-garden gathering after the preparation block. There are names beside the jobs that still need doing, rather than a demand to join. Kiyoko returns to Faculty Offices while Kana Fujimoto receives the first-years in the Lecture Hall.

Kana has set a small planted tray beside a copper warming charm. The tray’s owner wants the roots kept cool; another group needs the warm space for its work. A sketch of the shared bench lies between them.

“Before offering a compromise,” Kana says, “what would you need to know?”

Gaku pauses beside the door and revises his list. Bunta leans over to check whether the promised groups actually exist.

Several first-years begin inspecting the planted tray while another asks how long the charm must run. There is no agreed proposal yet, and no assigned spokesperson.'''),
}

STANDING = '''Teachers assess work they actually see and communicate what needs attention. Meeting expectations means the current work is adequate; needs improvement names a particular shortcoming and a feasible way to practice or revise; at risk follows serious misconduct or a continuing pattern of neglected responsibilities, not one awkward answer. These are qualitative judgments about the current course, not permanent labels for a person's worth. Feedback can be challenged with actual evidence. Successful repair changes later feedback and opportunities. Recommendations, supervised responsibilities and scarce placements depend on relevant work and conduct, without guaranteeing one outcome for every student.'''

ACADEMY_AI = '''Institutional duties: Phoenix Wing/red—defense and protective fieldwork; Gryffin Tail/cyan—research and investigation; Dragon Hoard/gold—exploration and relics; Hydra Head/violet—diplomacy and civic work. These do not sort personalities.
Four-year adult program: mandatory morning core lessons on the actual year timetable, plus assigned afternoon preparation, partner work and announced practicals, with breaks. Dialogue does not consume a day. Students can neglect duties and face proportionate consequences; never force their actions. Clubs are optional: invite through a person and a real activity. Outside work requires agreement and fits around class.
First Day establishes enrollment, timetable and welcome, then stops at a playable Magic Theory question. It leads to a supervised novice assessment. Keep actual student years; older helpers are not first-years. Headmasters return to Faculty Offices. Instructor starts give subject, room and tomorrow’s first-bell appointment. Faculty teach broad actual subjects, not affinity-only tracks.
Classmates pursue their own work and relationships: cooperate, bicker, fail, help and disagree with one another. The player joins an ensemble, not an audience arranged for their performance. Follow up only relevant actual exchanges; no reaction panel after each player action, automatic friendship, rivalry or prescribed ambition. Give concrete qualitative feedback and repairable setbacks. Routine classes need no extra quests. Archons represent; headmasters manage. Foreign fieldwork needs actual authorization, participation and truthful debrief; bystanders gain no credit. Nonstudents keep their paths. Pace major occasions through existing readiness. The thesis is one practical capstone per playthrough.'''

def apply(w):
    qs=w['parties']['']['quests'];new=[]
    for school,d in SCHOOLS.items():
        location=school+' Academy'
        start=next(v for k,v in w['storyStarts'].items() if k.endswith(school+': First Day'))
        start['description']=d['description']
        start['storyStart']=('[OPENING: Already-enrolled adult first-year, first teaching day. Establish the timetable, crowded welcome and routine walk into '+location+' - Lecture Hall. The named older students keep their actual years and are present as returning students/helpers, not first-years. The headmaster gives the welcome and returns to Faculty Offices. Then play the authored classroom question and the students’ approaches. STOP before the player chooses a partner, seat, answer, club or response; do not complete the exercise, assign emotions or imply a friendship. Use current character records and no automatic party recruitment. The near-term assessment is '+d['subject']+'. It is supervised beginner work using actual abilities, not a hidden high-level duel or a portal qualification.]\n\n'+d['scene'])
        first='First Morning at '+school
        # These are authored suggestions, not accepted employment or guild work.
        start['startingQuests']=[first,'Find Afternoon Work','Visit the Glowhaven Guild','Your First Spell']
        start['questGenerationGuidance']='The opening reaches the first Magic Theory question with the player still free to respond. Use '+first+' for genuine participation; an honest beginner mistake can qualify. The instructor then assigns '+d['assessment']+' as the near-term coursework, with a practical preparation step and a stated next lesson for assessment. Keep routine classes off the quest list. Optional spell invention, clubs and outside jobs remain available but are not automatically accepted. The older helpers retain their actual years. Do not invent the player’s motive, friendship or obligation to a voluntary job.'
        q=qs[first]
        q['questDesignBrief']='An enrolled adult first-year joins the opening Magic Theory question with '+d['teacher']+'. Resolve their actual participation before the lesson is completed. Observation, a safe test, reasoning or an honest attempt can contribute; failure does not require repeated enrollment. Classmates pursue their own methods and conversations. Let an exchange develop when the player actually joins it; do not make the class watch or evaluate every move. After the exercise, the instructor explains '+d['assessment']+', its scope, preparation and the next announced supervised session. Keep the assignment achievable for a novice, with no required new power or compulsory partner recruitment. Morning lessons and assigned afternoon work matter; breaks are available. Do not skip a term, award mastery or make every routine lesson a quest.'
        q['onCompleteEffects']=[{'type':'quest-init','operator':'set','value':d['assessment']},{'type':'quest-accept','operator':'set','value':d['assessment']},{'type':'story','instruction':'Complete only the actual first lesson and establish the course assignment '+d['assessment']+'. The teacher gives its next practical preparation step and the next announced supervised lesson, not an unannounced turn countdown. Continue classmates’ own work, conversations and unfinished concerns. If the player actually joined one, preserve that exchange without making the class revolve around them. Do not decide the player’s response, manufacture friendship, force a club or accept outside work. Continue the required timetable as appropriate; a conversation does not consume the day.'}]
        name=d['assessment'];new.append(name)
        qs[name]={'name':name,'questSource':d['examiner'],'questLocation':location,'detailType':'detailed','status':'hidden','questStatement':d['public'],'mainObjective':d['goal'],'questDesignBrief':d['brief']+'\n\nThis is coursework issued after the opening lesson; it has one native completion owner. Agree a modest preparation task and announce when the supervised assessment will happen through the scene. Do not require a literal multi-day wait, formal essay or exact words. '+STANDING+'\n\nLet classmates remember only what they experienced: a practical favor, disagreement, a promise or a contribution. A later saved seat or pointed comment should follow the actual exchange, not a preselected romance/rival. Finishing this introductory assessment does not complete the separate original-spell project, familiar exam, thesis or foreign fieldwork.', 'conclusive':True,
         'completionCondition':{'type':'story','query':'After receiving this assignment, the player actually prepares and takes part in '+d['subject']+' at '+location+', and the responsible instructor has reviewed the attempt and given concrete feedback. An honest unsuccessful attempt with intelligible feedback counts as finishing this assessment, without falsely calling it a pass. Merely hearing the assignment, promising to attend, watching someone else perform, claiming old work or leaving before participation and review does not complete it.'},
         'onCompleteEffects':[{'type':'player-experience','operator':'add','value':200,'target':'allPlayers'},{'type':'story','instruction':'Close '+name+' once with the actual result. The instructor gives specific qualitative feedback: what met expectations, what needs improvement, and a feasible next practice or revision. At-risk standing needs serious misconduct or an established pattern, not a single sincere failure. Show only reactions earned by the actual scene and mention an appropriate next ordinary class, personal follow-up or optional extracurricular without accepting it. Do not announce a pass, friendship, promotion, expedition seat or new ability merely because this quest ended.'}]}
        core=w['worldLore'][location+' — Core Subjects']['text']
        w['worldLore'][location+' — Core Subjects']['text']=core.replace('afternoons are normally free','afternoons include assigned preparation, partner work and announced practicals, alongside breaks and outside commitments')
        occasion=w['worldLore'][location+' — Academic Occasions']
        occasion['text']=occasion['text'].split('\n\nThe first near-term assessment is ')[0]+'\n\nThe first near-term assessment is '+d['subject']+'. '+d['public']+' Work and personal choices can affect whose advice students seek, how partners regard one another and which later opportunities they are prepared to pursue.'
        w['locations'][location]['basicInfo']=w['locations'][location]['basicInfo'].replace('Afternoons are normally free.','Afternoons include preparation, partner work and scheduled practicals, with breaks and optional activities around those responsibilities.')
        timetable=w['itemTypes']['First-Year Timetable - '+school]
        timetable['description']=timetable['description'].replace('Afternoons are normally free unless a specific supervised activity has been announced.','Afternoon preparation periods, partner work and supervised practicals are announced alongside the morning lessons, leaving room for meals, breaks and personal plans.')
    day=w['worldLore']['The Academy Day']['text']
    day=day.replace('Afternoons are normally free for lunch, clubs, supervised practice, study, friends, paid work or trips into the city. A previously announced examination, practical or field assignment can occupy additional time.','Afternoons include regular preparation, partner work, supervised practice and announced practicals. Instructors state the work and its next meeting clearly; students are expected to make time for it. Breaks, meals, friendships, optional clubs and outside work still fit around those responsibilities. A student may refuse or neglect work, but that choice can affect feedback, partners and later opportunities.')
    w['worldLore']['The Academy Day']['text']=day
    enrollment=w['worldLore']['Academy Enrollment and Daily Life']['text']
    w['worldLore']['Academy Enrollment and Daily Life']['text']=enrollment.replace('while afternoons normally leave time for lunch, clubs, supervised practice, study, friends and outside life. Examinations or announced practicals can need extra time.','and afternoons carry regular preparation, partner work and announced practicals, with breaks and time for outside life around those obligations. Clubs are optional and introduce themselves through people, open sessions and concrete activities. A student can try a club without joining it.')
    w['worldLore']['Academic Feedback and Opportunity']={'text':STANDING+'\n\nA defensive exercise can reveal whether someone looks after a partner. A research demonstration tests whether a claim survives an honest method. Field readiness can earn confidence without a reckless recovery. A civic exercise asks whether proposals can actually be carried out. These institutional emphases leave room for every discipline, martial method, temperament and chosen ambition.'}
    ai=w['aiInstructions']['generateStory'];k='Academy Life and Civic Mandates'
    ai[k]=ACADEMY_AI
    # Responsibility in the existing guild opening without assigning an unchosen job.
    guild=next(v for k,v in w['storyStarts'].items() if 'Adventurer: Guild Counter' in k)
    guild_scene='''Morning sun falls in green and honey-colored pieces across the guildhall tiles. At the appraisal bench beyond the counter, Karia Morino has propped her scythe safely clear of the aisle. Her coat is dusty, Renpei Sugita’s field map has dried into a curl, and Tetsuya Kanamori is examining a torn fastening with deep personal disapproval.

“The delivery arrived,” Renpei says. “We got everyone across.”

“After the cart stopped,” Karia replies, “you tried to lift it before checking what it was caught on.”

“I was trying to help.”

“You did help. After you stopped lifting it.”

Tetsuya holds the fastening up to the light. “And next time this comes to me before it tears. I charge less for a thing that is still one thing.”

Renpei begins defending the knot on his map case instead. Karia laughs, asks where he put their return note and starts checking her own pockets. Their difficult journey is over; settling its small consequences plainly is not.'''
    old='Morning sun falls in green and honey-colored pieces across the guildhall tiles. Someone at the request board is arguing that a delivery cannot possibly count as an expedition. The person beside him points at the distance written on the slip and keeps chewing her breakfast.'
    guild['storyStart']=guild['storyStart'].replace(old,guild_scene)
    guild['storyStart']=guild['storyStart'].replace('Present authored clerk: Komugi Hanabishi.','Present clerk: Komugi Hanabishi. At the visible appraisal bench, Karia Morino and Renpei Sugita are returning from an already-finished difficult delivery while Tetsuya Kanamori examines a damaged fastening. Stage these existing people for this opening without recruiting them, awarding the player their pay, creating a second commission or claiming an unplayed boss victory. They continue their own conversation rather than becoming an audience for the newcomer.')
    anchor='She rests her pen against the open ledger.'
    if 'find out who is depending on it' not in guild['storyStart']:
        guild['storyStart']=guild['storyStart'].replace(anchor,'“And when you do take a job, find out who is depending on it,” Komugi adds. “If the plan stops working, tell us. Someone can arrange help for a late delivery. They can’t arrange much for a person who simply disappears.”\n\n'+anchor)
    if 'Accepted work creates an actual commitment' not in guild['questGenerationGuidance']:
        guild['questGenerationGuidance']+=' Accepted work creates an actual commitment to a client, not a pile of simultaneous offers. Give the selected client a legible practical stake. Distinguish declining unaccepted work from abandoning an agreed job; proportionate responses and a chance to explain or repair depend on actual events. Do not confuse licensure with accepting a paid contract.'
    init=w['aiInstructions']['generateInitialStart']['Role Fidelity and Academy Years']
    init=init.replace('afternoons are normally free','afternoons carry assigned preparation and practical work with breaks').replace('Afternoons are normally free','Afternoons carry assigned preparation and practical work with breaks')
    init=init.replace('but stops before completing the first class.','and reaches the first teacher’s question, stopping before the player answers or completes the class.')
    w['aiInstructions']['generateInitialStart']['Role Fidelity and Academy Years']=init
    background=w['storySettings']['worldBackground']
    w['storySettings']['worldBackground']=background.replace('normally have afternoons free for study, work, practice and social life','balance assigned afternoon preparation and practical work with breaks, relationships and outside commitments')
    qs['Find Afternoon Work']['questStatement']='Rumor has it that some of Glowhaven’s inns, kitchens and other establishments could use a little extra help. It might be worth asking when your timetable allows.'
    qs['Find Afternoon Work']['questDesignBrief']=qs['Find Afternoon Work']['questDesignBrief'].replace('After morning classes,','When the player has time around classes and assigned preparation,')
    qs['Visit the Glowhaven Guild']['questStatement']='Rumor has it that the Adventurer’s Guild welcomes curious visitors as well as licensed adventurers. Its public hall could be a useful place to hear about life beyond the academy.'
    qs['Visit the Glowhaven Guild']['questDesignBrief']=qs['Visit the Glowhaven Guild']['questDesignBrief'].replace('after morning classes or whenever the player chooses','whenever the player chooses and has time around their actual obligations')
    return {'rewrittenAcademyStarts':4,'newNativeAssessments':new,'newMechanicalTriggers':0,'newGradeResources':0,'retainedSuggestedLeads':['Find Afternoon Work','Visit the Glowhaven Guild']}
